Transcript corrupts under BIT_GC=stress against a Bit-sourced libbitrt.a.
#1845 IS CLOSED AND THIS FILE MUST STAY. That ticket fixed the deterministic
`bridge=0/31` and the intermittent SIGSEGV — an unguarded `rootInit` whose
`rootBindMemory` had already repointed the live collector's heap — and said so
in its own scope note: "The waiver covers ONLY the stress-policy mask. The
deterministic 0/31 and the intermittent SIGSEGV are fixed in code, not waived."
An earlier version of this line read "Tracked as #1845, which must remove this
file when it closes", which would have deleted a live waiver the moment that
ticket closed. Removing this marker needs a way to root a raw port address
across an allocation — a change to the object model, not the closing of a bug.

Measured 7 mismatches in 30 runs. The corruption is in the STRING BYTES, which
is what identifies the cause:

    expected   P3[panic: boom
    got        P3[panic: \xef\xbf\xbd\xef\xbf\xbdm

and less often the type name in P7m, where a swept Circle's memory came back as
a Square:

    expected   panic: type assertion failed: Circle is not Square
    got        panic: type assertion failed: Square is not Square

Both are the same defect as ../rootio's waiver, in the same shared module. This
program drives ../rootio/props' `ioProps`, which holds every port object as a
raw `int` - `mine` returns an ABI.md §2 header address, and nothing here is
reachable from a reference-typed slot. Once the program shares the live heap,
any allocation can collect a string the transcript has not written yet.

There is nowhere to root them. Every port object in that module is an `int`,
including the `rtSliceNew` handle inside `mine`, which is deliberate - it tests
the object model below the type system.

The default-policy run still asserts the whole transcript byte for byte, and
that is the run this program was written for: it checks that a provider puts the
right bytes on the right file descriptor, not that the collector traces them.

STILL OWED: ../rootiolinux drives the same `ioProps` and will fail the same way,
but it is linux-only (unbuildable on this host) so it has NOT been measured and
is deliberately NOT waived here. A waiver without evidence is a silent loss of
coverage; let the Linux gate confirm it rather than assuming it.
